  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Burundi - Projet conjoint de chemin de fer &#xe0; &#xe9;cartement standard Tanzanie/Burundi/RDC &#x2013; Phase II</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Burundi - Tanzania/Burundi/DR Congo Joint Standard Gauge Railway Project Phase II</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Le chemin de fer est construit pour transporter des passagers de l&apos;industrie mini&#xe8;re, de l&apos;agriculture et des marchandises g&#xe9;n&#xe9;rales. Les b&#xe9;n&#xe9;ficiaires imm&#xe9;diats du projet seront donc les exploitants miniers, les agriculteurs et la population en g&#xe9;n&#xe9;ral, qui b&#xe9;n&#xe9;ficieront d&apos;un acc&#xe8;s &#xe0; des services de transport efficaces, et les gouvernements du Burundi et de la Tanzanie, qui profiteront de l&apos;exploitation commerciale des richesses mini&#xe8;res.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On the Burundi side, the project target area covers Gitega and Rutana provinces, with an estimated population of about 500,000 people, of whom 50.3% are women. The railway section will run from Malagarasi to Musongati mine fields, where significant freight sidings, the Burundi SGR control station and a marshalling yard will be developed. The immediate beneficiaries include mine operators, farmers, traders, passengers, and the general population, who will gain access to more efficient and lower-cost transport services. The Government of Burundi will benefit through improved trade access via the Tanzanian SGR network and the Port of Dar es Salaam, while the project is also expected to stimulate mining development at Musongati, agricultural production, and cross-border trade. Additional direct beneficiaries include Burundian institutions and workers, particularly through technical assistance to the future Autorit&#xe9; de R&#xe9;gulation du Transport Ferroviaire (ARTF), training for about 1,500 Burundians in railway-related fields, and social infrastructure support, including the rehabilitation of Rutana Hospital.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">La pr&#xe9;sente intervention est relative au Projet Conjoint de Chemin de Fer Tanzanie - Burundi SGR Phase II CRC. Le projet est con&#xe7;u afin de renforcer l&apos;int&#xe9;gration r&#xe9;gionale et de tirer profit des march&#xe9;s int&#xe9;gr&#xe9;s et des &#xe9;conomies d&apos;&#xe9;chelle r&#xe9;sultant d&apos;un d&#xe9;veloppement et d&apos;une exploitation conjoints, la Tanzanie, le Burundi et la R&#xe9;publique d&#xe9;mocratique du Congo (RDC) d&#xe9;veloppent un programme de chemin de fer &#xe0; &#xe9;cartement standard (SGR) par &#xe9;tapes, centr&#xe9; sur le port de Dares-Salaam et le corridor de transport central. Le projet comprend six (06) composantes principales : (i) Conception et construction du lot 6 : Tabora - Kigoma SGR (Tanzanie) ; (ii) Conception et construction du lot 7 : Uvinza - Malagarasi SGR (Tanzanie) ; (iii) Conception et construction du Malagarasi - Musongati SGR (Burundi) ; (iv) Renforcement des capacit&#xe9;s et assistance technique ; (v) Appui &#xe0; l&apos;&#xe9;quipe conjointe de coordination du projet (ECP) ; (vi) Consultations pour les &#xe9;tudes strat&#xe9;giques et la sensibilisation ; et (vii) Mise en &#x153;uvre du projet. Le co&#xfb;t du projet est estim&#xe9; &#xe0; 3 929,46 millions d&apos;USD, y compris 12,5 % d&apos;impr&#xe9;vus financiers et 12,5 % d&apos;impr&#xe9;vus physiques. La section tanzanienne co&#xfb;tera 3 209,27 millions d&apos;USD, tandis que la section burundaise co&#xfb;tera 720,19 millions d&apos;USD. Le projet contribue au financement climatique, avec une enveloppe de 80 millions USD du montant total approuv&#xe9; d&#xe9;di&#xe9;e &#xe0; l&#x2019;att&#xe9;nuation.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Tanzania/Burundi/DR Congo Joint Standard Gauge Railway Project Phase II &#x2013; Burundi Section is the Burundian component of a multinational regional railway project designed to improve transport connectivity along the Central Corridor and strengthen regional integration and trade. For Burundi, the project focuses on the construction of the 84 km Malagarasi&#x2013;Musongati electrified standard gauge railway (SGR), linking Burundi to the Tanzanian SGR network and, through it, to the Port of Dar es Salaam. The Burundi section also includes supervision services, technical assistance and capacity building, strategic studies, social and environmental sensitization and monitoring, RAP implementation, and the rehabilitation of Rutana Hospital. The total cost of the Burundi section is estimated at USD 720.19 million, financed by African Development Fund (ADF) grant resources of USD 23.67 million, Transitional Support Facility Pillar I grant resources of USD 15.78 million, ADF Regional Operations grant resources of USD 59.17 million, and a Government of Burundi counterpart contribution of USD 621.57 million. The project contributes to climate finance, with 80 million USD of the total approved amount dedicated to mitigation.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">L&apos;objectif de d&#xe9;veloppement du projet est d&apos;am&#xe9;liorer la connectivit&#xe9; des transports le long du corridor central afin de renforcer l&apos;int&#xe9;gration r&#xe9;gionale et le commerce et d&apos;encourager l&apos;exploitation mini&#xe8;re et agricole &#xe0; grande &#xe9;chelle gr&#xe2;ce &#xe0; l&apos;acc&#xe8;s &#xe0; des services de transport ferroviaire de grande capacit&#xe9;.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The development objective of the Burundi section is to improve transport connectivity along the Central Corridor in order to enhance regional integration and trade, and to incentivize large-scale mining and farming through access to high-capacity railway transport services. More specifically, for Burundi, the project seeks to construct the Malagarasi&#x2013;Musongati SGR section, connect Burundi to the Tanzanian railway network and the Port of Dar es Salaam, and build the institutional, legal, regulatory, technical and human capacity required for the creation and future operation of Burundi&#x2019;s railway sector.</narrative>
  </description>
